  <h2>Output formats</h2>
  <p>
    Commands that return a collection print an aligned table; commands that return one
    record print a field block. <code>--json</code> replaces either with the pretty-printed
    API response, which is what you want when piping into <code>jq</code>.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`# A field block
appmctl builds get 019fc789-47b5-7034-9291-4cd3c57b75d5

# The same record as JSON, for scripting
appmctl --json builds get 019fc789-47b5-7034-9291-4cd3c57b75d5 | jq -r '.data.status'`} />

  <h2>Exit status</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Successful operations exit <code>0</code>.</li>
    <li>
      Commands exit non-zero on local validation errors, <code>curl</code> failures, non-2xx
      HTTP responses, invalid JSON requested through <code>--json</code>, and output errors.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>stream</code> passes through the wrapped command's status. If the command
      succeeds but a log upload or managed status update fails, it exits <code>1</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>builds wait</code> uses dedicated codes so a CI job can gate on a build:
      <code>0</code> completed, <code>1</code> failed, <code>130</code> cancelled,
      <code>124</code> timed out.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>--dry-run</code> validates configuration and arguments, then prints the request
      plan without sending anything.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For unsuccessful responses the body is printed to stderr when available, followed by
    the status:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Status</th><th>Common cause</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>401</code></td><td>Invalid or revoked API key</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>403</code></td><td>Missing API scope or team permission</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>404</code></td><td>Wrong team/app/build path, or a cross-team resource</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>409</code></td><td>Manual writes attempted on an automatic build, or a conflicting terminal state</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>422</code></td><td>API validation failure</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The client does not retry. Wrap a retry around an operation only when it is safe or
    explicitly idempotent — build creation is idempotent when the same
    <code>--request-uuid</code> is reused.
  </p>

  <h2>Current boundaries</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>stream</code> works only with builds in <code>manual</code> reporting mode; provider-reported builds reject manual status and log writes.</li>
    <li>Output is combined stdout/stderr. Separate streams and terminal control sequences are not preserved.</li>
    <li>The API accepts at most 256 KiB per log chunk and 5 MB per complete build log. The CLI does not truncate for you.</li>
    <li>A response is treated as bytes unless <code>--json</code> is passed; content type does not change output behavior.</li>
    <li>There is no config file or credential store. Configuration is explicit through environment variables and flags.</li>
    <li><code>builds list --status</code> is accepted but has no effect: the API's build index takes no status filter.</li>
    <li><code>builds when-running --timeout</code> is accepted but has no effect; see <a href="/cli/builds">Builds</a>.</li>
    <li>Notification destinations have no API, so no CLI command covers them; see <a href="/cli/integrations/notifications">Notifications</a>.</li>
    <li><code>credentials verify</code> transmits a verdict your build reached; the CLI does not resolve an external vault itself. See <a href="/cli/credentials">Credentials</a>.</li>
  </ul>
